  "summary": "This paper evaluates annual satellite embeddings from AlphaEarth Foundations V1 (geographic mosaic) and TESSERA v1 for screening one-year tree-cover transitions (gains and losses) in the conterminous United States (CONUS), using human-interpreted labels from the LCMAP evaluation benchmark (2017-2021) and an Annual NLCD Collection 1.0 extension (2020-2023) with initial cover classes supplied. The authors test whether adjusting reference-forest cosine similarity scores via year-matched (concurrent) references or independent control-plot mean subtraction improves classification performance (Youden's J at a transferred 10% false-positive rate threshold calibrated on earlier years and non-overlapping regions) relative to a fixed start-year reference direction and supervised classifiers. They find that reference and control adjustments alter only 1.5-9.2% of pooled decisions, yield high rank correlations with unadjusted scores (Spearman 0.947-0.996), and show no consistent improvement in Youden's J (-0.037 to +0.011), with all eight primary correction contrasts flipping signs across alternative calibration splits. More substantively, the study reveals a stark asymmetry between one-year tree loss and tree gain: while unsupervised cosine change magnitude strongly discriminates tree loss (AUROC 0.968 for AlphaEarth, 0.956 for TESSERA), it detects 0 of 24 primary tree gains and ranks gain plots below unchanged grass/shrub negatives (AUROC 0.287-0.318 overall; 0.339-0.352 against unchanged-only negatives), whereas supervised classifiers and subsequent-state (or even pre-transition state) scores achieve much higher gain discrimination.",
